{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-253548",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The infimum of a set $D$ is the greatest lower bound of $D.$\n\nIf $D$ is a finite set, the infimum of $D$ equals its minimum.\n\nFirst, let's write down all the terms of our set by substituting $n=1,2,3,4$ into the expression for the elements of $D:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2-{2}^{2} & =-2 \\\\ 3-{3}^{2} & =-6 \\\\ 4-{4}^{2} & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nD=\\{0,\\,-2,\\,-6,\\,-12\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}inf(D) & =min\\{0,\\,-2,\\,-6,\\,-12\\}=-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
